      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(slack): log typing reaction errors instead of silently swallowing them",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nThe typing reaction (emoji) add and remove calls both silently swallow all errors via empty `.catch(() => {})` handlers. If the bot token lacks `reactions:write` scope, the configured typing reaction never appears and the operator has no way to diagnose why.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThe existing error handling matches no known Slack error recovery pattern — `already_reacted` / `no_reaction` (handled elsewhere in the codebase) are the only expected reaction errors that warrant suppression. All other errors (missing_scope, invalid_auth, etc.) should be visible so operators can diagnose configuration issues.\n\n## User Impact\n\nOperators who configure a `typingReaction` emoji without realizing their token lacks `reactions:write` will now see `slack send: typing reaction failed: missing_scope` in verbose logs instead of silent failure.\n\n## Evidence\n\n**Existing tests still pass (84/84):**\n\n```\n Test Files  1 passed (1)\n      Tests  84 passed (84)\n```\n\n**Live Slack proof (transient, not committed):**\n\nWith a bot token that lacks `reactions:write`, the configured typing reaction silently fails:\n```\n→ Before fix:  (no output — error swallowed)\n→ After fix:  \"slack send: typing reaction failed: missing_scope\" appears in verbose logs\n```\n\nThe proof is reproducible with any Slack bot token that has `reactions:read` but not `reactions:write`.\n\n## Review\n- Manually reviewed and verified\n- AI-assisted: Yes",
